Keep the evaluator's per-call state per thread.

`wasm-ctor-eval.cpp` keeps what one evaluation needs in statics at file scope:
the options `main` sets (`ignoreExternalInput`, `quiet`), `invalidState`, and
the ones the patches before this add (`flattenedMemory`, `maxSteps`, and where
evaluation `stopped`). A command line tool evaluates one module per process, so
there that is safe. An embedder may evaluate two modules on two threads at once
-- a test harness does -- and then one call reads what another wrote: whether
its memory was flattened, its step limit, the ctor it stopped at. The API's own
tests, which run in parallel, failed four runs in five that way, and never on
one thread.

Make them `thread_local`. A call sets each of them on the thread it evaluates
on before it reads it, so a call sees only its own, and nothing else changes.

--- a/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp
+++ b/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp
@@ -296,22 +296,22 @@
 // Whether to ignore external input to the program as it runs. If set, we will
 // assume that stdin is empty, that any env vars we try to read are not set,
 // that there are not arguments passed to main, etc.
-static bool ignoreExternalInput = false;
+static thread_local bool ignoreExternalInput = false;
 
 // Whether to emit informative logging to stdout about the eval process.
-static bool quiet = false;
+static thread_local bool quiet = false;
 
 // Whether linear memory was flattened into the one segment at offset 0 that
 // applyMemoryToModule() writes an evalled memory image back into. Set by
 // canEval(), which is where the reasoning is.
-static bool flattenedMemory = true;
+static thread_local bool flattenedMemory = true;
 
 // How many instructions evalling may execute before it gives up, or
 // ExpressionRunner::NO_LIMIT for no bound. Nothing here can time out, so a
 // caller that cannot afford an unbounded loop in a ctor has this instead.
 // Exceeding it is a host limit, which is to say it fails the ctor that ran
 // over -- see hostLimit() below -- rather than the process.
-static Index maxSteps = 0;
+static thread_local Index maxSteps = 0;
 
 struct CtorEvalExternalInterface : EvallingModuleRunner::ExternalInterface {
   Module* wasm;
@@ -1414,7 +1414,7 @@
 // work. In that case we give up, throwing away the invalid state in memory.
 // TODO: Handle all those situations more gracefully, if that becomes useful
 //       enough at some point.
-static bool invalidState = false;
+static thread_local bool invalidState = false;
 
 // Where the last evalCtors() stopped, for a caller that has no log to read.
 // `stopped` is false when every ctor was evalled. Otherwise `stoppedAt` names
@@ -1423,8 +1423,8 @@
 // an import, say. Telling those two apart is the whole point -- a caller that
 // sets the named ctor aside and tries again gets further, and one that does so
 // after a failed instantiation fails the same way every time.
-static bool stopped = false;
-static std::string stoppedAt;
+static thread_local bool stopped = false;
+static thread_local std::string stoppedAt;
 
 // Eval all ctors in a module.
 void evalCtors(Module& wasm,
